AI Usage Guidelines — Draft v1 (Claude for Code, Gemini for Documents)
Section 1 — What AI tools we plan to use, and what we will use each for
We will use Claude (via API or claude.ai) to generate boilerplate CRUD endpoints and first-draft unit tests — not for anything touching authentication or payment logic, which a human writes from scratch.
We will use Claude for quick syntax checks and one-off debugging questions — not as a substitute for human review before code is merged to main.
We will use Claude to brainstorm and compare architecture options before we commit to a design — not to make the final call; that's a team discussion, recorded in DECISIONS.md.
We will use Claude to generate an initial test skeleton for a new module — a human adds edge cases and confirms coverage before it counts as done.
We will use Gemini to draft the first pass of docstrings, API documentation, and README content — a human always reviews and edits before it's merged.
We will use Gemini to draft and revise other project documents (e.g. meeting notes, spec write-ups, user-facing guides) — not to make final wording decisions on anything binding, like DECISIONS.md entries, which a human writes.
We will not use either tool's output as a substitute for writing the PR description or DECISIONS.md entry ourselves.
Section 2 — How we will document AI interactions
Every prompt used to generate code that ends up in the repo goes in the prompt engineering log: the prompt text, the model used (Claude Sonnet 4.6 / Haiku), and what we kept vs. changed.
Every prompt used to generate documentation content that ends up in the repo or shared docs also goes in the prompt engineering log: the prompt text, the model used (Gemini), and what we kept vs. changed.
One-off debugging questions, syntax lookups, or quick phrasing suggestions don't need a log entry — only prompts that produced code, text, or decisions that ended up in the repo.
The PR description's "what this PR does" field always names whether Claude or Gemini was involved, even in one sentence — it's not a copy of the full log entry, just a pointer to it.
If a Claude or Gemini suggestion changed our actual approach — for example, Claude proposed a different data model, or Gemini's draft reframed a spec's scope — that's a DECISIONS.md entry, not just a prompt log entry.
Section 3 — How we will handle disagreements about AI output quality
If two engineers disagree about whether Claude-generated code is good enough to merge, the code steward has final say — not whoever wrote the prompt, and not whoever has more experience.
If two members disagree about whether Gemini-generated document content is good enough to publish or merge, the doc steward has final say — not whoever wrote the prompt, and not whoever has more experience.
Evidence required for a code merge decision: passes the existing test suite, passes the PR review checklist, and can be explained by someone other than whoever generated it.
Evidence required for a document to be finalized: accurately reflects the team's actual decisions (cross-checked against DECISIONS.md), and can be explained by someone other than whoever generated it.
If the disagreement is about style or preference rather than correctness/accuracy, we defer to the team's existing style guide (for code) or documentation template (for docs) rather than debating it case by case.
A vote is not evidence. If the team can't resolve it using the criteria above, the relevant steward's call is final, and gets one sentence in DECISIONS.md explaining why.
